We need to examine the legal framework: Moldovan Assemblies Act (sections 6,7,8,11,12,14,15). Also the Code of Administrative Offences (Article 174 §§). The authorities acted based on the claim that the demonstration lacked proper authorization because the municipal council said the judgment was not final. The law requires prior notification to the municipal council (Section 11) and the municipal council may reject only if convinced after consulting police that sections 6 and 7 would be breached. The municipal council gave a decision granting authorization for 1 Aug only. The applicants sought a longer period, got a court order. The municipal council refused to implement the court order because it wasn't final. The police then arrested them. The question: Was the interference prescribed by law? The law does allow police to intervene if an assembly is unauthorized. The authorities claimed the demonstration was unauthorized because the municipal council hadn't issued an authorization. However, the court had ordered the municipal council to do so. The Supreme Court later quashed the appellate judgment, but that happened after the demonstration. At the time of the demonstration, the appellate judgment existed but was not final due to Supreme Court pending. So the legal situation is complex.

